  "Question": "What are the coordinates of the intersection of the lines \\(y=x+1\\) and \\(y=-x+3\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "(1,2)",
  "Accepted Answers": [
    "(1,2)",
    "（1，2）",
    "(1，2)",
    "（1,2）"
  ],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Equating the two expressions gives \\(x+1=-x+3\\), so \\(x=1\\). Substitution gives \\(y=2\\), so the intersection is \\((1,2)\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Add to eliminate x\n\nAt the intersection, both equations hold for the same \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Their \\(x\\)-terms are opposites, so adding the equations eliminates \\(x\\):\n\\[\n2y=(x+1)+(-x+3)=4.\n\\]\nThus \\(y=2\\). Substituting into \\(y=x+1\\) gives \\(2=x+1\\), so \\(x=1\\). The intersection is \\((1,2)\\).",
